<h2>What the 2015 amendment changed</h2>
<p>The original Section 17 allowed a tribunal to order a party to take interim measures of protection, but it stopped there: no enforcement mechanism, no parity with the court's powers, no consequence for defiance. The Arbitration and Conciliation (Amendment) Act, 2015 substituted the provision with two operative limbs. Section 17(1) confers on the tribunal, during the arbitral proceedings, the power to grant the full range of interim measures — the same kinds of protection a court grants under Section 9. Section 17(2) supplies what was always missing: the tribunal's order is deemed to be an order of the court for all purposes and is enforceable under the Code of Civil Procedure, 1908 as if it were an order of the court.</p>
<p>The amendment's design is visible when the two limbs are read with Section 9(3), inserted at the same time: once the tribunal is constituted, the court shall not entertain a Section 9 application unless the Section 17 remedy would not be efficacious. Parliament's intent was to move interim relief into the tribunal's hands for the duration of the arbitration, keeping the court as the forum of first resort only before the tribunal exists and as a backstop afterwards.</p>

<h2>The tribunal's toolkit under Section 17(1)</h2>
<p>The measures available track the familiar Section 9 heads: appointment of a guardian for a party under disability for the purposes of the proceedings; preservation, interim custody or sale of goods that are the subject matter of the dispute; securing the amount in dispute, including by directing deposits or furnishing of security; detention, preservation or inspection of property, with powers of entry and sampling; interim injunctions; appointment of receivers; and a residual power to grant such other interim measures of protection as appear just and convenient. In commercial practice the workhorses are orders securing the amount in dispute, injunctions restraining dealings with disputed property or invocation of contractual rights pending the award, and preservation orders over perishable or deteriorating subject matter.</p>
<p>Timing matters: the Section 17 power operates during the arbitral proceedings. For the period before the tribunal is constituted, the party's recourse is the court under Section 9 or, in institutional arbitrations, an emergency arbitrator — a mechanism examined in a separate article on this site.</p>

<h2>Enforcement and appeal: the two exits from a Section 17 order</h2>
<p>A party armed with a Section 17 order and facing non-compliance takes the order to the competent court for enforcement under Section 17(2), where it is executed in the same manner as the court's own interim orders, with the same consequences for disobedience. A party aggrieved by the order — whether by its grant or its refusal — has the appeal under Section 37(2)(b). The appeal lies to the court defined under the Act, and the scheme admits no second appeal. Appellate interference is restrained in practice: the tribunal's assessment of a prima facie case, balance of convenience and irreparable harm is treated as a discretionary evaluation, disturbed only for perversity or misapplication of principle. The practical message for parties is that the Section 17 hearing before the tribunal is the main event, not a dress rehearsal.</p>

<h2>The structural limit: third parties</h2>
<div class="note"><p>An arbitral tribunal draws its authority from the arbitration agreement, and its orders therefore bind only the parties to that agreement. It cannot injunct a bank from honouring a guarantee, restrain a sub-purchaser from dealing with goods, or direct a depository, registrar or government authority to act. Wherever the needed relief must operate against such third parties, Section 17 cannot deliver it, and Section 9(3) itself recognises the situation: the Section 17 remedy is not efficacious, so the court's door reopens.</p></div>
<p>This limit is the single most important input into forum choice. It is also a drafting input: a party whose likely interim needs involve third-party instruments — guarantees, letters of credit, escrow arrangements — should assume court involvement at the interim stage and structure the dispute clause accordingly.</p>

<h2>Court or tribunal: the tactical matrix</h2>
<table class="law">
  <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Better forum</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Relief needed before the tribunal is constituted</td><td>Court (Section 9)</td><td>Section 17 operates only during the proceedings; the tribunal does not yet exist.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Relief against the counterparty after constitution</td><td>Tribunal (Section 17)</td><td>Section 9(3) bars the court unless Section 17 is inefficacious; the tribunal knows the record and moves within the arbitral calendar.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Relief against banks, transferees or other non-parties</td><td>Court (Section 9)</td><td>The tribunal cannot bind third parties; this is the paradigm Section 9(3) exception.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Confidential subject matter</td><td>Tribunal</td><td>Arbitral proceedings stay private; court filings and orders are public record.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Immediate coercive execution expected</td><td>Either, with planning</td><td>A Section 17 order is enforceable as a court order, but enforcement still requires a trip to court; where defiance is likely, factor in that extra step.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Compliance mechanics in practice</h2>
<p>Well-advised parties build compliance into the order itself. Applications under Section 17 should seek time-bound, verifiable directions — deposit within a stated number of days into an identified account, disclosure on affidavit by a named officer, status reports to the tribunal at fixed intervals — rather than open-textured restraints, because a precise order is easier to enforce under Section 17(2) and harder to evade plausibly. Where a deposit or security is directed, the order should specify the mode and custodian. And because the tribunal retains control of the proceedings, persistent non-compliance can be brought back before it: the tribunal may take the conduct into account, adjust interim arrangements, and the compliant party may simultaneously pursue enforcement through the court. The combination of a watchful tribunal and the deemed-court-order status of Section 17(2) has turned a once-toothless provision into the ordinary channel for interim protection in Indian arbitration.</p>
